# netra agent configuration. Copy to .env next to compose.yaml and fill in the
# two required values.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Every variable is NETRA_-prefixed. That is deliberate: the agent runs with
# network_mode: host, where bare names like TOKEN or LISTEN collide with whatever
# else the box exports.
#
# Variables below are marked either CONSUMED TODAY (internal/agent/config/
# config.go reads them now) or NOT YET CONSUMED (specified in §6.6, but no code
# reads them yet — setting one today has no effect and produces no error).

# --- Required ----------------------------------------------------------------

# CONSUMED TODAY. Base URL of the hub, scheme included. The agent POSTs to
# <NETRA_HUB_URL>/api/agent/v1/ingest. The agent refuses to start without it.
NETRA_HUB_URL=https://netra.example.com

# CONSUMED TODAY. The agent's bearer token, minted by the hub and belonging to
# exactly one host. Starts with `nta_`. The agent refuses to start without it.
#
# The hub stores only a SHA-256 of this string, so a lost token cannot be
# recovered — it is replaced. Mint one in the hub's host UI, at
# http://127.0.0.1:8080/ on the hub host, or over the admin API with a POST to
# /api/v1/hosts carrying the admin token as a bearer.
NETRA_TOKEN=

# --- Scrape interval ---------------------------------------------------------
#
# There is no variable here, and that is not an omission. The scrape interval is
# a fixed 60s constant (config.ScrapeInterval). The hub has no per-host cadence
# column, so it could only ever hand every agent back the same hardcoded value,
# silently overriding whatever an operator had set locally on the first
# successful flush. A knob the hub can override behind your back is worse than
# no knob, so there is none. A genuine per-host override belongs to the admin
# API in phase 2. Per-collector intervals (SMART at 1h, for example) are a
# separate mechanism and are listed further down.

# --- Host identity: operator-stated facts ------------------------------------
#
# All four are CONSUMED TODAY and all four are optional. The agent performs no
# geocoding and makes no outbound calls other than to the hub: it states a fact
# and the hub interprets it. These live in the agent's env because the person
# deploying the host is the one who knows where it physically is.
#
# setup-agent.sh asks for location, provider and host type. It does NOT ask for
# facility: a datacentre code most self-hosters do not have would be a prompt
# nobody can answer, which teaches people to hit Enter through the rest of them.

# e.g. Gravelines, FR
NETRA_LOCATION=
# e.g. OVH, Hetzner, on-prem
NETRA_PROVIDER=
# e.g. GRA11 — the specific datacentre/rack/room
NETRA_FACILITY=
# bare_metal | vps | vm
NETRA_HOST_TYPE=

# --- Buffering ---------------------------------------------------------------

# CONSUMED TODAY. How much backlog the in-memory ring buffer holds while the hub
# is unreachable. Default 1h.
#
# Hard-capped at 6h, and the agent refuses to start above that. The cap is the
# hub's continuous-aggregate start_offset: data buffered longer than that and
# then replayed lands in a chunk TimescaleDB no longer re-materialises, so it
# would be silently excluded from every rollup forever. Failing loudly at
# startup beats graphs that quietly disagree with the raw table.
#
# The buffer is memory only and is intentionally not durable across a restart.
NETRA_BUFFER_WINDOW=1h

# --- Logging -----------------------------------------------------------------

# CONSUMED TODAY. debug | info | warn | error
NETRA_LOG_LEVEL=info

# --- Filesystem roots --------------------------------------------------------

# CONSUMED TODAY. Where the agent looks for procfs and sysfs. Leave both alone
# in the shipped compose: they exist so the collectors can be pointed at a
# fixture tree in tests, not because a normal deployment needs to move them.
NETRA_PROC_ROOT=/proc
NETRA_SYSFS_ROOT=/sys

# CONSUMED TODAY. Where the logged-in session count is read from. Only
# meaningful when /var/run/utmp is bind-mounted into the container; the users
# collector reports the file as unavailable and leaves the metric NULL
# otherwise, which is also the normal outcome on Alpine and other busybox
# systems that ship no utmp writer.
NETRA_UTMP_PATH=/var/run/utmp

# --- PID namespace -----------------------------------------------------------

# CONSUMED TODAY. Whether this container runs with `pid: host`.
#
# The process collector otherwise has to guess whether it is confined to a PID
# namespace, and every guess has a case it gets wrong — so state the fact. Set
# it to 1 only if compose.yaml actually has `pid: host`; claiming it falsely
# makes the agent report its namespace's two processes as the host's total.
# setup-agent.sh writes this to match what it rendered.
NETRA_PID_HOST=0

# --- NOT YET CONSUMED --------------------------------------------------------
#
# Specified in §6.6 of docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-07-netra-design.md. The
# collectors that read them have not landed — today CPU, memory, load,
# kernelstat, netstat, procs and users exist. They are listed here so the shape
# is settled, and left commented out so
# nobody is misled into thinking a set value is doing something.

# NOT YET CONSUMED. Comma-separated collector names to disable, e.g.
# smart,processes. One uniform mechanism rather than a boolean per collector.
# NETRA_COLLECTORS_DISABLED=

# NOT YET CONSUMED. How often SMART is polled. Much slower than the 60s scrape
# because spinning up a passthrough query per drive every minute is pointless.
# NETRA_SMART_INTERVAL=1h

# NOT YET CONSUMED. Explicit device:type list, overriding `smartctl --scan -j`.
# The type follows the device after a colon, so no separator variable is needed.
# NETRA_SMART_DEVICES=/dev/sda:sat,/dev/nvme0:nvme

# NOT YET CONSUMED. Two explicit lists rather than a magic `-` prefix on one:
# an include list and an exclude list, both by chip_name+label.
# NETRA_SENSORS=
# NETRA_SENSORS_EXCLUDED=

# NOT YET CONSUMED. Per-read deadline for a single hwmon file. A hung
# temp1_input on a flaky driver must not stall the whole scrape.
# NETRA_SENSORS_TIMEOUT=2s

# NOT YET CONSUMED. Overrides automatic primary-sensor selection. Automatic
# selection prefers known CPU chips (coretemp, k10temp, zenpower, then thermal
# zones) rather than hottest-wins, which otherwise surfaces a warm disk as the
# host's headline temperature.
# NETRA_PRIMARY_SENSOR=
